WSL (Ubuntu) Setup & Run Guide — CSCI 473 Thermal Stencil 2D

This guide walks through setting up WSL: Ubuntu on Windows, cloning our repository, installing the required C/OpenMP/MPI build tools, and compiling/running the 2D thermal simulation.

1. Install or Open WSL (Ubuntu) on Windows

Open PowerShell or Windows Terminal (as Administrator if WSL isn't installed yet).

If you do not have Ubuntu installed yet, run:

wsl --install -d Ubuntu


(Restart your computer if prompted, then launch Ubuntu from the Start menu and create your Linux username and password.)

If WSL: Ubuntu is already installed, launch Ubuntu from the Start menu (or connect in VS Code by clicking the blue >< icon in the bottom-left corner and selecting Connect to WSL).

2. Install Required Compilers & Tools in Ubuntu

Inside your Ubuntu terminal, update your package list and install gcc, make, git, and OpenMPI (mpicc / mpirun):

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y build-essential git openmpi-bin libopenmpi-dev


Verify everything is installed properly:

gcc --version
mpicc --version
nproc


(Note: nproc shows how many logical processors your machine has so you can keep worker counts within safe hardware limits without oversubscribing.)

3. Clone the Repository

Navigate to a Linux home directory (recommended for fast I/O, e.g., ~/projects) or your workspace folder and clone the repo:

mkdir -p ~/projects
cd ~/projects
git clone https://github.com/AndrewEMorgan05/csci473-thermal-stencil-2d.git
cd csci473-thermal-stencil-2d


(If VS Code is connected to WSL, you can open this folder directly with code . from the terminal.)

4. Compile & Run the Current Baseline (make-2d, print-2d, stencil-2d)

Move into the code/ folder and compile:

cd code
make


(If you ever get a carriage-return error from Windows line endings, run sed -i 's/\r$//' Makefile *.c *.h and then make.)

Ensure the data/ directory exists and run a quick 5x5 test:

mkdir -p ../data
./make-2d 5 5 ../data/init_5x5.dat
./print-2d ../data/init_5x5.dat
./stencil-2d -t 5 -i ../data/init_5x5.dat -o ../data/out_5x5.dat -v 2


Run a 16x16 test:

./make-2d 16 16 ../data/init_16x16.dat
./stencil-2d -t 20 -i ../data/init_16x16.dat -o ../data/out_16x16.dat -v 1


CLI Flag Reference

-t <num_iters> (or -n <num_iters>): Number of stencil iterations/time steps

-i <input_file>: Input binary .dat grid file

-o <output_file>: Output binary .dat grid file

-v <0|1|2>: Verbosity (0 = silent/errors only, 1 = basic info & timings, 2 = print grid at every iteration)

-p <workers>: Number of workers/threads (used in parallel versions)

5. Compiling & Running Parallel Versions (As We Add Them)

Pthreads (stencil-2d-pth.c): Compile with -pthread and run:

./stencil-2d-pth -t 100 -i ../data/init_512.dat -o ../data/out_512_pth.dat -p 4 -v 1


OpenMP (stencil-2d-omp.c): Compile with -fopenmp and run:

./stencil-2d-omp -t 100 -i ../data/init_512.dat -o ../data/out_512_omp.dat -p 4 -v 1


MPI (stencil-2d-mpi.c): Compile with mpicc and launch via mpirun:

mpirun -np 4 ./stencil-2d-mpi -t 100 -i ../data/init_512.dat -o ../data/out_512_mpi.dat -v 1


Hybrid MPI + OpenMP (stencil-2d-hybrid.c): Compile with mpicc -fopenmp and combine OMP_NUM_THREADS with mpirun -np:

OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 mpirun -np 2 ./stencil-2d-hybrid -t 100 -i ../data/init_512.dat -o ../data/out_512_hyb.dat -v 1


6. Daily Git Sync Workflow

Before editing files each day, pull the latest commits:

git pull


When you are ready to share updates:

git status
git add .
git commit -m "Describe your update"
git push